WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:05.080
When scared or threatened, mammals can
respond in different ways.

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:08.720
They can freeze, fight or run away.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:11.740
Now scientists have been studying mice

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:16.480
to identify the brain regions involved in
this split-second decision.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:25.780
They showed the mice a looming overhead threat.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:29.120
The mice responded in the typical ways

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:29.820
either:

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:33.120
by freezing

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:39.540
seeking shelter

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:47.560
or aggressively rattling their tails.

00:00:49.739 --> 00:00:53.670
The scientists monitored the mices' brain
activity during the experiment

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:56.460
to find the pathways involved in the different responses.

00:00:57.220 --> 00:01:00.060
They found that mice who had been scared before

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:03.320
were more likely to respond aggressively.

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:06.440
This reinforcement of aggressive behaviour is a survival
mechanism but it could malfunction.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:16.020
In humans, exploring this mechanism could
help researchers understand conditions

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:21.900
like PTSD and phobias.

